this  isn't a batman movie at least not like  the ones we've become accustomed to  seeing but in a way the batman actually  fixed the superhero genre as a whole and  entirely changed the way that many  people feel about dc movies instead of  the typical hollywood blockbuster batman  formula that we've seen time and time  again we got a highly subjective and  creative hero film focused on the  character of bruce wayne which is  exactly what we needed  [Music]  before we get started here's a spoiler  warning for the batman we will be  talking about lots of spoilers in the  film so please wait to watch this video  until after you've seen it if you don't  want it to be spoiled now that that's  out of the way you might be wondering  how exactly is the batman not a batman  movie and to answer that we'll have to  discuss a few things about the film it's  sound design it's cinematography  romanticism and character also we're  going to talk about how the movie kind  of fixed the superhero genre and gave us  a new take on where the genre should be  headed first things first though we have  to talk about the film's sound this  movie was brought to life not just by  visuals but by its sound design and  mixing the soundtrack and sound design  was used to perfection and this  shouldn't come as a shock because the  film's director matt reeves is well  known for his intense focus on the sound  design for his films such as the planet  of the apes movies as well as the  cloverfield franchise both of which have  unique and utterly striking sound  production the film's composer michael g  aquino is also well known for his work  on pixar movies like ratatouille and  coco as well as big budget action films  such as star wars rogue one and avengers  end game and i think most people can  agree that ratatouille coco and end game  have some of the best musical  compositions out of any of their similar  films so gia kino's spectacular work on  the batman comes as no surprise two  specific pieces about the film's sound  design which truly set it apart from the  batman films we've been inundated with  over the past few decades are its  soundtrack and the batmobile in recent  years dc has been known for taking pop  songs and just plugging them into scenes  where they really aren't necessary see  2016's suicide squad for reference the  batman though actually treats its  soundtrack with care giving us songs  which fit the tone of the movie  perfectly and which actually makes sense  for the scenes in which they play the  song ave maria for example is played  over certain scenes with the riddler and  the tone of the song truly showcases the  insanity of his character while still  making sense for him as it would have  been a song he sing as a choir boy in  the wayne orphanage the second piece of  the film sound design which has helped  to show where superhero sound design  should be going in the future is the  batmobile this incarnation of the  batmobile is easily the least flashy of  all the batmobiles we've ever seen and  it was meant to be that way it's not a  tank or a futuristic machine it's a  handcrafted super powerful muscle car  and it certainly sounds like one there's  actually a prequel novel to the film  called before the batman which explains  how bruce began building this  incarnation of the batmobile when he was  just 16 constantly trying to find new  ways to make it faster the sound and  production designers of the film did a  fantastic job bringing this idea to life  with a striking build and an even more  striking engine sound the production and  sound designers on the film were given  lots of creative freedom when making the  batman and it really shows in the  finished product which is all the more  evident in the film's cinematography  this movie is probably one of my  favorite pieces of cinematography since  2015's the revenant the film's head  cinematographer greg frazier has always  produced stunning work most notably in  the mandalorian rogue one and dune  frazier was given a ton of creative  freedom to make the film unique and  different from all the cgi-infused  superhero films which typically dominate  the box office there are plenty of  unique shots present in the movie which  lesser cinematographers would have shied  away from but frazier went at them  straight on to give us a picture of what  personal superhero stories should look  like brutal traumatic and in the  batman's case grungy there are two shots  which i think exemplify the film's  superior cinematography with the first  being after bruce flips the penguin's  car rather than giving us a third person  omniscient view of the events unfolding  we see the menacing figure of the batman  walking towards us from the penguins  perspective giving us a feeling of being  trapped and utterly terrified the other  shot is when the batman is fighting some  goons in a pitch-black hallway lit only  by the flashes of gunfire this scene is  reminiscent of the hallway scene in  rogue one in which darth vader cuts down  an entire squadron of rebel soldiers  scenes like this just show how the  creators weren't concerned with how much  money the film was going to make but  instead focused on how they could tell a  compelling story in the most creative  way possible most of all though the  batman lets us see the world through  bruce's eyes sometimes even literally  with the help of his contact lens  cameras seeing things from bruce's  perspective differs heavily from the way  most batman movies have told their  stories in the past rather than a fourth  wall view of the happenings on screen we  get to see inside the mind of bruce  wayne just like we saw in the comics  which brings us to our next point  romanticism the batman seems like a love  letter to the best batman stories not to  the films that came before but to the  comic stories which kicked off the  intense batman fanbase the film somewhat  romanticized some of the darker and more  iconic batman comics such as hush and  the long halloween in doing so they were  able to make one of the best stories  since christopher nolan's the dark  knight or the animated dark knight  returns the film reaffirms the love and  nostalgia for batman without taking it  in the exact same direction we've seen  over and over rather than following in  the footsteps of ben affleck's batman  robert pattinson's takes on a gritty and  grungy approach that isn't cringy for  once it's realism at its height it's the  most believable of all the batman we've  seen and it doesn't waste any time with  the batman origin story that we've seen  so many times now that almost everyone  can tell exactly when the pearl necklace  will be ripped romanticism is a  subjective art form and the batman did a  spectacular job of bringing the  subjective hero story from comic book  pages and placing it directly onto the  silver screen none of this would have  been possible though without character  going into this movie i was expecting  yet another unoriginal batman story with  a twist cedric diggory is a bat but  instead of a watered-down boring take on  the same batman we've seen over and over  we got a dark and gritty story focused  on the character behind the mask bruce  wayne this film stuck to its main  objective in the story unlike the dark  knight rises which tried to accomplish  too much in too little time  similar to the dark knight rises gotham  city is seen as a character of its own  too but it's done much better this time  we actually get to see the people in the  heart of the city as they go through  traumatic events and how they recover  rather than just giving us a third  person omniscient view of the events  happening around them the film actually  gives a surprisingly compelling social  commentary about the division of  economic classes and how the ritually  often only care about themselves the  riddler and his gang of followers coined  the phrase no more lies which is just as  much of a commentary on the  socio-economic environment of the film  as it is a critique of the superhero  genre as a whole the creators of the  film recognize that audiences have  become somewhat frustrated with the  current limits of the superhero genre  and the continual stream of carbon copy  superhero films which are released so  instead of rehashing the same batman  story we've seen time and time again we  got no more lies we got a hardcore  vigilante whose brutality complicates  the heroics we usually associate with  him we got a traumatized and angry  orphan just trying to make the city a  better place most of all though we got  the world's greatest detective the same  bat that originated in ink on paper so  i'll say it again this isn't a batman  movie it's a bruce wayne movie or a  detective movie or better yet it's a  very welcome and realistic approach to  the batman character unlike any batman  movie we've ever seen before  anyways that's going to be all for today  if you liked what you saw maybe check  out the video on your screen for some  different content i make videos on  basically anything that interests me in  the world of entertainment also don't  forget to like and subscribe as it truly  does help me out and thanks for watching  rocket riley  you

